WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.402
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.234 --> 00:00:13.080
NARRATOR: September 6, 2017.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:19.350
A flash of ultraviolet and
X-ray light erupts from the sun,

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:22.260
sending a burst of
radiation toward Earth.

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:26.490
We call it a solar flare.

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:29.790
HAKEEM OLUSEYI: Flares are
huge explosions on the surface

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:33.120
of the sun throwing light
out into the solar system

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:35.350
and absolutely baking
anything in its path.

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:38.910
 These are really powerful
events erupting from the sun.

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:40.800
And we are fundamentally
in the line of fire.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.300
NARRATOR: The flare
is so intense it

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.760
causes radio blackouts
across the side

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.560
of the Earth facing the sun.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.500
And the timing could
not have been worse.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.120
PHIL PLAIT: The problem
was at this time

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:00.890
there were hurricanes
in the Atlantic

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:02.720
bearing down on the Caribbean.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:05.540
And there were people trying to
get in there and rescue folks.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:11.910
NARRATOR: Radio communication
between disaster relief teams

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:13.670
goes down for most of the day.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:20.125
Space weather and Earth weather
combined to create chaos.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:26.150
HAKEEM OLUSEYI: It's not
just an academic exercise.

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:29.870
Your very lifestyle
depends on understanding

00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:31.010
how the sun behaves.

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:34.310
We need to be able
to predict what

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:36.080
the sun is going to do next.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:38.870
And in order to do that, we
need to have eyes on the sun,

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:40.925
as many eyes as we can get.

00:01:40.925 --> 00:01:45.260
CONTROL TOWER: 3, 2, 1.

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:48.840
NARRATOR: That's why NASA has
put together an elite unit.

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:52.220
CONTROL TOWER: We have
ignition, and we have liftoff,

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:55.700
liftoff, and liftoff on
an international mission

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:56.680
of solar fittings.

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:00.890
NARRATOR: A fleet of
daring spacecraft.

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:02.870
 We humans have
come a long way.

00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:05.150
If you go back a
few thousand years,

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:06.920
people were worshipping the sun.

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:10.190
And today, we've built
a fleet of spacecraft

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:13.130
that monitor the
sun 24 hours a day,

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:16.160
observing it from
every angle, predicting

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:17.780
what it's going to do next.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:18.580
That's nuts.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:22.590
NARRATOR: Leading
the pack is the Solar

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:24.945
Dynamics Observatory or SDO.

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:29.560
It keeps a telescopic
eye on flares blasting

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:30.595
out from the sun's surface.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:36.720
 24 hours a day, the SDO
probe is on the lookout.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:38.780
It's constantly vigilant.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:40.290
PHIL PLAIT: It looks
at the sun at a lot

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:43.410
of different wavelengths
in exquisite detail

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:46.620
so that we can see exactly
what sort of high-energy events

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:47.810
are happening on the sun.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:51.560
NARRATOR: The record
for the longest service

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:56.270
goes to SOHO, our sentinel
monitoring the sun

00:02:56.270 --> 00:02:59.120
and its faint outer atmosphere.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:00.560
PHIL PLAIT: The Solar
and Heliospheric

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:04.280
Observatory or SOHO is the
old hand at studying the sun.

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:07.100
It's been up there
for 25 years observing

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:10.800
not just the sun itself but
the environment around it.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.770
NINA LANZA: Instead of
looking directly at it,

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:14.720
it actually blocks
out most of the light

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:17.240
so that we can see all
these beautiful structures

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:18.530
that surround the sun.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:23.810
NARRATOR: And then
there's the new recruit,

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:27.930
the Parker Solar
Probe, our scout

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:29.625
going deep behind enemy lines.

00:03:30.826 --> 00:03:32.460
PAUL SUTTER: The
Parker Solar Probe

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:36.240
is going to give us the
closest look at the sun

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:40.320
that we have ever had in
the history of humanity.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.600
 We're flying so
close to the sun,

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:46.590
we're flying through the
gases of its atmosphere.

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:49.890
NARRATOR: 29 other craft
make up the fleet each

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:52.170
with their own role to play.

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:54.120
PHIL PLAIT: This
fleet of spacecraft

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.220
that we have, the
overall mission is

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:58.350
to observe the sun
scientifically,

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:00.480
learn about its
behavior, but also

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:04.190
importantly to learn about the
effects that it has on Earth.

00:04:07.990 --> 00:04:09.670
NARRATOR: Our look-out
probe, the Solar

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:13.630
Dynamics Observatory or SDO,
spots something hellish.

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:19.050
PHIL PLAIT: Today's
forecast for the sun

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:21.870
is predicted to be 10,000
degrees Fahrenheit with winds

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:25.870
of up to 600 miles per
second and a pretty

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:27.130
decent chance of rain.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.000
HAKEEM OLUSEYI: You don't
want to be in this rainstorm

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.740
because your umbrella
isn't going to help you.

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:34.890
Solar rain isn't water.

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:36.930
It's plasma, hot plasma.

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:42.060
NARRATOR: Rather than a
solid, liquid, or gas,

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:45.450
the entire sun is made up
of a strange fourth state

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:48.115
of matter, plasma.

00:04:53.905 --> 00:04:57.910
MICHELLE THALLER: Plasma is just
simply gas that's very hot, so

00:04:57.910 --> 00:04:59.650
hot it's lost some
of its electrons

00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:01.360
and become electrically charged.

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:03.130
And the wonderful
thing about plasma

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:05.110
is that once it has
that electric charge,

00:05:05.110 --> 00:05:07.020
you can direct it
with a magnetic field.

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:11.670
NARRATOR: And the sun has the
most powerful magnetic field

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:12.630
in the solar system.

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:16.950
It twists and flows
through the entire star.

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:22.780
 The sun's magnetic field
is incredibly complicated.

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:25.222
There are lots of loops
of magnetic lines coming

00:05:25.222 --> 00:05:26.055
out of the sun's surface.

00:05:29.328 --> 00:05:31.570
GIBOR BASRI: As you
go above the surface,

00:05:31.570 --> 00:05:35.140
the magnetic field begins to
control where the gas can go.

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:38.050
And you get these beautiful
loops and other structures

00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:40.450
above the sun that
are just showing you

00:05:40.450 --> 00:05:41.620
where the magnetic field is.

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:47.470
NARRATOR: The SDO spacecraft's
jaw-dropping videos

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:51.220
of rain on the sun show
plasma flowing along

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:54.760
huge, looping magnetic field
lines that have punched

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:55.720
through the sun's surface.

00:05:57.020 --> 00:05:59.150
PHIL PLAIT: Because the
surface of the sun is a plasma,

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:01.880
and these loops of magnetic
fields are coming out of it,

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:05.240
it draws that plasma up,
which then cools and rains

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:06.800
down as a plasma rain.

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:11.680
NARRATOR: Tracking these
loops of magnetic field

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:14.170
and plasma could help
explain solar flares.

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:19.860
And it could give
us some warning.

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:25.830
September 2017.

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:30.930
Minutes before the devastating
mid-hurricane radio blackout,

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:33.330
our solar look-out
spotted an explosion

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:35.550
on the surface of the sun.

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:38.100
PHIL PLAIT: Suddenly, you have
this tremendous flow of energy

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:40.240
and an explosive release of it.

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:42.150
And we call this a solar flare.

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:46.260
This is SDO's specialty
of observation.

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:48.120
NARRATOR: The craft
tracked the magnetic loops

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:49.200
above the sun's surface.

00:06:51.730 --> 00:06:52.795
They were highly unstable.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:58.110
 SDO takes very
detailed pictures

00:06:58.110 --> 00:07:00.000
of where the flows are.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.820
You can actually see gas
flowing from one place.

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:05.190
And you can see the-- you
can see the magnetic loops.

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:10.340
PHIL PLAIT: This magnetic flux
loops are kind of like wires

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:11.630
carrying electricity.

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:13.380
When they're on their
own, they're fine.

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:16.280
But if they get too close, they
can connect and short circuit,

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:18.530
and then you get a
tremendous release of energy.

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:22.800
 The Ghostbusters were
really on to something.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:23.865
Don't cross the streams.

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:31.230
NARRATOR: The contact between
magnetic loops released an epic

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:33.330
explosion of high-energy light.

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:38.483
This radiation altered
our planet's atmosphere.

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:43.740
Radio waves jammed,
causing blackouts

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:45.290
at the worst possible moment.

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:49.070
PHIL PLAIT: Solar flares
are happening on the sun

00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:51.010
90 million miles away.

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:52.330
Who cares, right?

00:07:52.330 --> 00:07:53.590
Well, think of it this way.

00:07:53.590 --> 00:07:57.430
A really good solar flare could
be the equivalent of, oh, say,

00:07:57.430 --> 00:08:00.310
10 million hydrogen
bonds going off.

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:01.450
How's that sound?

00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:05.290
Solar flares happen a lot,
and they do affect us.

00:08:05.290 --> 00:08:07.400
They have affected
us in the past,

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:08.590
and they will again
in the future.

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:12.040
NARRATOR: But the sun's
twisting magnetic fields

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:14.470
do more than just disrupt
our communications.

00:08:16.383 --> 00:08:17.550
HAKEEM OLUSEYI:
When we see flares,

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:19.540
we got to keep an
eye out on those.

00:08:19.540 --> 00:08:21.420
But sometimes, there's
something even bigger.

00:08:23.890 --> 00:08:26.320
NARRATOR: The sun launches
huge clouds of plasma

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:27.630
across the solar system.

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:31.210
PHIL PLAIT: All of
that material can

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:34.030
come to the Earth and
really mess things up here--

00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:38.080
blow out our power grid, destroy
our satellites, even physically

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:39.520
harm our astronauts in orbit.

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:43.810
NARRATOR: Can the solar
fleet safeguard Earth?

00:08:43.810 --> 00:08:46.560
[MUSIC PLAYING]

